# The tens to a hundred and the numbers between them, read off digit cards and on price tags.
lesson language-to-a-hundred v=1 format=teach grade=5 unit=1 subject=language levels=[easy, medium, hard] {
  title "Numbers to [[num.one-hundred]]"
  goal "Say numbers to a hundred in [[language.name]] and use them for prices."

  look {
    scene 37x8 {
      column all at=canvas(1, 1) space=1 align=start {
        row tens space=2 align=end {
          digitcards a digits=[4, 0]
          digitcards b digits=[7, 2]
          digitcards c digits=[1, 0, 0]
        }
      }
    }
    say """
      The tens: [[num.forty]] (40), [[num.fifty]] (50), [[num.sixty]] (60), [[num.seventy]] (70),
      [[num.eighty]] (80), [[num.ninety]] (90) and [[num.one-hundred]] (100). A number between the
      tens is the ten, and, and the one: [[num.seventy-two]] is 72. Sixty and seventy sound alike,
      so listen for the middle of the word.
      """
  }
  do {
    level easy {
      worked language.to-a-hundred-name k=5
    }
    show language.to-a-hundred-name k=3 easy-k=0 hard-k=7
    show language.to-a-hundred-meaning k=3 easy-k=2 hard-k=4
    show language.to-a-hundred-write k=4 easy-k=1 hard-k=6
    show language.to-a-hundred-name k=5 easy-k=2 hard-k=8
    show language.to-a-hundred-match k=1 easy-k=0 hard-k=2
    show language.to-a-hundred-write k=6 easy-k=3 hard-k=4
    show language.to-a-hundred-listen k=3 easy-k=0 hard-k=5
    show language.to-a-hundred-say k=1 easy-k=0 hard-k=2
    level hard {
      show language.to-a-hundred-write k=3
    }
  }
  language es {
    try stars=2 {
      show language.es-to-a-hundred-why k=1 easy-k=0 hard-k=2
    }
  }
  try stars=3 {
    show language.to-a-hundred-puzzle k=1 easy-k=0 hard-k=2
  }
  remember {
    say "[[num.forty|cap]], [[num.fifty]], [[num.sixty]], [[num.seventy]], [[num.eighty]], [[num.ninety]], [[num.one-hundred]]. Between the tens: [[num.seventy-two]]."
  }

  grown-ups """
    The wrong idea to watch for is sixty and seventy swapped, and a number between the tens said as
    two separate numbers. The digit cards carry the number, so the child's job is the word. From
    this grade the written words are marked with their accents: a word typed without its accent is
    not taken. The dictation is read from this sheet, where it prints as Read aloud, or played by
    the device. If the child is stuck, count in tens together, then add a one: forty, forty-one. Off
    the page, read the prices in a shop window in the language. It meets England's key stage 2
    languages programme (years 3 to 6, statutory from age 7): "explore the patterns and sounds of
    language through songs and rhymes and link the spelling, sound and meaning of words"; Japan's
    grades 5 and 6 foreign languages: "Activities to listen to concrete information regarding
    familiar and simple matters in daily life such as dates, times and prices etc."; and CEFR A2,
    "Can understand sentences and frequently used expressions related to areas of most immediate
    relevance (e.g. very basic personal and family information, shopping, local geography,
    employment)". The phrases are in [[language.variety]].
    """
  level easy {
    grown-ups "At this level [[num.seventy]] is worked first in the teacher's pen, the words to name are [[num.forty]] and [[num.one-hundred]] and the words to write are [[num.fifty]] and [[num.ninety]], the plainer ones of the lesson, with the first set to match, the shortest sentence to hear and the first version of each star question."
  }
  level hard {
    grown-ups "At this level the words to name are [[num.forty-eight]] and [[num.seventy-two]] and the words to write are [[num.eighty]], [[num.sixty]] and [[num.ninety]], the ones most often mixed up, with the last set to match, the longest sentence to hear, a third word to write and the last version of each star question."
  }
}
